<div class="sales">

  <div class="section">
    <p class="label">The Zero Fog</p>
    <h1>Why You Lose 2-3 Sharp Hours Every Day - And How to Get Them Back</h1>
    <p class="lead">By the end of this, you'll understand why you lose two or three hours of sharp focus every single day.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Do the math. Two to three hours a day, twenty working days a month - that's forty to sixty hours. A full work week, gone. Every single month. Multiply that by twelve, and it's four months of sharp thinking a year. Just... gone. I call it the productivity tax. You pay it every month, and you never even see the bill.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You'll see the hidden pattern that's been getting worse every week. You'll understand why no productivity tool, no habit, no "just push through" ever fixed it - and why none of it was your fault.</p>
    <p class="body-text">A quick word first. I'm not here to build a brand. I'm not here to sell you a personality. I'm here to share what actually works. The story you're about to read is real. The losses are real. And the system I found is real - built on peer-reviewed clinical science.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Your morning. See if this sounds like you.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">You don't set an alarm. Or maybe you do, and ignore it. You wake up at a different time every day. Monday at nine. Wednesday at eleven. Friday - you don't even check.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You figured you'd shake it off by noon. You didn't. Two PM, brain fog hits. You re-read the same Slack message three times and still don't know what it says. You switch to easy tasks - anything to avoid real thinking. Your third cup of coffee isn't working anymore. You've hit a wall, and you know the rest of the day is going to be a struggle.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You forgot what a hundred percent feels like.</p>
    <p class="body-text">And the worst part? You stopped noticing. Being tired became your normal. You don't say "I'm exhausted" anymore. You say "I'm fine." But you're not fine. You're running at sixty percent. Every single day. And you don't even realize it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>It's not discipline. It's your brain.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Think about your workday. You work eight, ten hours - and let's be honest, when you work from home it's rarely just eight. But how many of those hours are actually sharp? Maybe three. Four if you're lucky. The rest, you're just... there. At your desk. Going through the motions.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Here's what most people miss: it's not because you lack discipline. Your prefrontal cortex - the part of your brain that handles focus, judgment, planning, impulse control - is the first thing that shuts down when your brain hasn't fully recovered. That's not a character flaw.</p>
    <p class="body-text">What nobody talks about: you can't fix this by working more hours. Working ten hours instead of eight doesn't make you more productive - it makes you more exhausted tomorrow. You're not underperforming because you're not working enough. You're underperforming because your brain can't use the hours you already have. The problem isn't the quantity of hours. It's the quality of what your brain does with them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>You think it's burnout. It isn't.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">You've probably told yourself this is burnout. Too much work. Too much stress. You need a break. So you take one. A long weekend. A week off. Maybe longer. I took a year off. A full year. And nothing changed. I rested - and still felt destroyed.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Here's what I didn't understand at the time. I thought if I just removed the load - the pressure, the deadlines, the work - I'd recover. That's what everyone thinks. But removing the load and actually recovering are two completely different things.</p>
    <p class="body-text">It's like tearing a muscle. You don't fix a torn muscle by lying on the couch longer. A torn muscle needs targeted rehab - specific exercises, in the right order, at the right time. Without that, it just heals wrong. A vacation is the wrong tool for the job. Because "burnout" is the label we put on it. It's not the cause.</p>
    <p class="body-text">If time off actually fixed this, you'd already be fine. Think about your last vacation. Did you come back sharp? Or did the fog come back within days?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>It's not a bad week. It's a cycle - and it's getting stronger.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Low energy leads to more caffeine. More caffeine leads to an afternoon crash. Now, everyone dips between two and four PM - that's basic physiology, everyone has it. But when you're already running on empty, the dip becomes a crash you can't work through. And the cycle repeats. Every day, a little worse. Every week, a little harder to break.</p>
    <p class="body-text">And caffeine itself turns on you. After a few weeks of daily coffee, it almost stops giving you energy. And the tolerance builds. You need more to feel the same, until even three cups barely moves the needle.</p>
    <p class="body-text">If any of this sounds familiar - stay with me. The next part is where it starts to make sense. Because I went through all of it myself. And it took me three years to find the way out.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>My story. It's probably a lot like yours.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">During the pandemic I had to find a remote job. At first I was happy - I could work from home, from my room. Life was good. But the schedule was brutal. Work ended at eleven PM, sometimes one AM. There was no fixed end time. I had no time to recover, and my schedule fell apart. Every morning I woke up feeling destroyed.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Over time I got angry and snappy from being tired. By evening I was too drained to do anything good with my day. I was drinking coffee nonstop - my cup was a bottomless pit. No energy for exercise. No energy for real food. Every day I burned out a little more. The company was great - good people, good culture, support at every step. But that wasn't enough. Because only I could fix what I had broken. At some point I burned out completely. And that's how my career ended. After almost three years.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Then I finally decided to build the business I'd been thinking about for years. Studied hard, did everything right. But I had no energy. Brain fog every day. I cut corners everywhere, and it all fell apart. Like stepping on the same rake again and again. I kept ignoring the one thing that mattered - my body.</p>
    <p class="body-text">So I decided to just stop. Stop everything and rest. But rest didn't help. One month turned into six. Six turned into a year. A full year of doing nothing. No energy for friends - even texting back felt like a big deal. No energy for my wife - conversation felt like an obligation. I couldn't even play video games, and I loved games. I'd nap twice a day. And the cycle repeated.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Then everything changed - and I didn't know why</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Then my mom came to visit. We walked, went to the beach, spent time in the sun. I was exhausted by evening - but a different kind of exhausted. No room for afternoon naps. And within days, everything shifted. I started going outside on my own. I started talking to my wife because I wanted to, not because I had to. I started actually replying to my friend's messages. I felt like a different person. But I had no idea why.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Then she left. For a week or two I held on to that rhythm. The naps came back. The fog came back. And that's when it hit me: whatever had happened while she was here, it wasn't willpower. It wasn't motivation. Something about those weeks had reset something in my body. And without knowing what it was, I couldn't keep it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>I decided to figure it out myself</h2>
    <p class="body-text">I started going on evening walks, trying to hold on to at least that one habit. And I made a decision: I'm going to figure this out myself. Nobody was going to hand me the right answer.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I started researching. Health podcasts, performance optimization, anything I could find. Hundreds of results. Most of it was garbage - the same recycled advice over and over. Wake up at 5 AM. Cold plunges. Meditate. Nothing that explained what had actually happened to me during those weeks with my mom. So I kept digging.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I filtered by medical professionals only - no bloggers, no influencers, no AI slop. And that's when I found it. A podcast with an actual specialist. Not a wellness coach - a clinician. A doctor who specializes in why the brain stops performing, and what actually fixes it. I pressed play. And by the end of that walk, everything started making sense.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I went deeper - the books, the lectures, the whole clinical system. Almost none of it had ever been translated to English. That's why you've never heard any of this. You didn't miss it - it was never there to find. Putting it all together into one system took months of work. And what I found was completely different from everything else out there. Not generic tips. Not ten things to try. Actual clinical protocols. Clear systems for what to do, what not to do, and in what order. That's what was missing from everything I'd tried before.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I couldn't stop. Every podcast, every interview, every lecture, every paper this doctor had ever published - some of them two or three times. I learned why I always lacked energy. Why I got tired so fast. Why rest alone never fixed anything. And I started applying it. Small things. And I felt the difference - not in weeks. In days. Fewer bad thoughts. More energy. I started wanting to do things again.</p>
    <p class="pull">Everyone is walking around broken - and nobody told them it can be fixed.</p>
    <p class="body-text">It felt like I took NZT from the movie Limitless. I could see what to do with my life and how to do it. I just go - task after task. When stress hits, I act instead of spiraling. Solutions that used to be hidden behind a wall of fog are just there now. Things I'd put off for months started getting done in hours. Not because I suddenly had superhuman discipline. Because my brain was finally running at full capacity.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>The real reason nothing worked</h2>
    <p class="body-text">I was the perfect customer. I bought everything, followed every step. And nothing worked. Not because I lacked discipline - but because none of it was designed to fix the real problem.</p>
    <p class="pull">They sold me band-aids when I needed surgery.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>You've been fixing the symptoms</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Here's what I want you to see. You've been fixing the symptoms - productivity apps, focus hacks, morning routines. But none of that touches the cause. They're aimed at the wrong layer. I know, because I tried all of them.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Maybe you even went further. Bloodwork - normal. Thyroid panel - normal. Vitamin D, B12, iron - all fine. Great news... that explains nothing. You're "healthy on paper" and still running on fumes.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I tried building the perfect morning routine. I watched Mark Wahlberg talk about his "4 AM Workout Club". Then YouTube exploded with people trying to copy that, like waking up at four AM is the secret to success. I tried Pomodoro. I tried Notion setups, task managers, to-do apps - and spent more time organizing my productivity system than actually being productive. I got sucked into the whole productivity porn rabbit hole. Cold plunges at six AM. Supplements - magnesium, probiotics, that green powder subscription every podcast swears by - whatever the next guru was selling. Journaling every morning because that's what "high performers" do. Cutting gluten. Cutting dairy. Keto, fasting windows - months of food experiments, because maybe the fog comes from your gut.</p>
    <p class="body-text">I'm not saying any of that is garbage - some of it makes sense. But none of it moved the needle. Not for me, not for anyone I know. Right now I'm building this entire project without any of it. No Pomodoro. No fancy task manager. No supplements. Just a brain that actually works when I sit down.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Because the problem was never the tools. The problem was what was running underneath. And what runs underneath is sleep.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Step back for a second. Your focus runs on your energy. When your brain doesn't recover at night, everything falls apart the next day. You're not lazy. You're not burning out. You've just been trying to fix the top of the chain while the bottom was broken. And it comes down to three systems. When they work together, everything works. When they don't, nothing does.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Everything maps to one of three</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Think about it this way. Every single thing you've experienced with your sleep comes down to one of these three.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You work until two AM, wake up at seven for a call, then wonder why you can't fall asleep at midnight the next day. That's the Clock. Your body lost track of when night is.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You're on your third coffee by noon and it still doesn't work, and by four PM your brain just shuts down. That's the Battery. You keep blocking the signal that tells your body it needs rest, and now the signal doesn't come through.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You finally get to bed at a decent hour, exhausted - and the second your head hits the pillow, you start solving every problem from the day. Emails, calls, decisions. You lie there for hours. That's the Off-Switch. Your brain learned that bed is where you work, not where you rest.</p>
    <p class="pull">Three systems. Not five, not ten. Everything maps into one of these three.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Here's what the research actually found - and this is the part nobody tells you. A good night isn't one thing. It runs on exactly three conditions. Your body has to expect sleep at that hour - that's timing. It has to actually need sleep - that's pressure. And your brain has to be able to let go - that's the switch. Break any one of the three, and the night falls apart - it doesn't matter which one. And there is no fourth.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Look back at what you just read - the two AM nights, the third coffee that stopped working, the racing brain on the pillow. Every one of them is one of these three breaking.</p>
    <p class="body-text">That's not my framework - that's how sleep is built. Science has known this for decades. It just never made it into the productivity blogs. I didn't pick the number. Biology did.</p>
    <p class="body-text">When they're out of sync, you get enough hours but zero rest. Exhausted but wired. Brain fog all day. This is why you can sleep seven or eight hours and still feel terrible - still foggy by noon, still dragging, still wondering why everyone else seems to have more energy. The hours aren't the problem. The systems are.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Why nothing you tried worked</h2>
    <p class="body-text">Everything was aimed at one piece. Maybe two. Never the foundation. Your morning routine is a partial Clock adjustment at best. Pomodoro and focus apps don't touch any of the three. Melatonin nudges the Clock slightly and does nothing for your Battery or Off-Switch - that's why it works for a week and stops. "Turn off your phone before bed" is one tiny piece of the Off-Switch, with two systems still completely broken.</p>
    <p class="body-text">And the tracker - the ring, the watch? It was never built to pull. Its job is to measure. It tells you your deep sleep was bad, and that's where its job ends. Maybe it hands you a tip - drink less coffee, keep the room cool. Generic advice, same for everyone. What it can't tell you is which of YOUR three systems is broken, in what order to fix them, and what to change tonight. And look - if any of it was going to fix this for you, it already would have. You're here because it didn't. That's what a broken base does: every tool gives you back a few minutes. All three. In the right order. Starting with knowing which one is broken - for you.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Productivity coaches tell you to build better routines, better workflows, better habits. And you did. It didn't work - not because the advice was wrong, but because it was aimed at the wrong layer. Sleep books give you three hundred pages of why sleep matters and ten pages of what to do. Random tips aimed at random systems. No order. No protocol. Puzzle pieces without the picture on the box.</p>
    <p class="pull">It's not that you failed. The approach was incomplete.</p>
    <p class="body-text">You were solving one piece of a three-piece problem. That's why the cycle doesn't break - you address one system, and the other two keep spinning the wheel.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Resetting all three doesn't require sacrifice</h2>
    <p class="body-text">You don't need to wake up at five AM, unless you want to. You don't need to give up coffee forever. You don't need to meditate every day, buy expensive gadgets, follow a rigid schedule, or spend hours on bedtime rituals.</p>
    <p class="body-text">What you actually need:</p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li>Fifteen minutes a day</li>
      <li>Something that works with any schedule, not against it</li>
      <li>Progressive - one action per lesson</li>
      <li>A system. Not willpower.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pull">You don't need more discipline. You need a system that runs without it.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Now you know why your brain has been running at sixty percent: three systems out of sync. And why no productivity hack or coffee fix ever solved it: an incomplete approach. Let's talk about what the fix actually looks like.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>One thing per lesson - not twenty</h2>
    <p class="body-text">You don't need more tips. You've had tips your entire life. Turn off your phone. Drink chamomile tea. Tips are random - no order, no sequence. Most programs dump twenty things on you on day one. This one gives you one thing per lesson. You stack them over four weeks.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>The question isn't whether. It's when.</h2>
    <p class="body-text">I've given you the cliff notes: the three-system model, why nothing worked. But the cliff notes aren't the protocol. Before I show you the full thing, think about something. You've seen the pattern. You know it doesn't break on its own. How long have you been saying "I just need more discipline"? "It's just a bad week." "I'll get it together next Monday." I said that for three years. The question isn't whether to fix this. It's when.</p>
    <p class="body-text">And for the first time, this was built for people who work from home. Not a hospital program. Not advice for nine-to-five workers. Built for the chaos of no fixed schedule, no commute, no separation between work and rest.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <p class="label">The Protocol</p>
    <h2>Let me show you what's inside</h2>
    <p class="body-text">This is The Zero Fog. And to be clear: this isn't a sleep course. It's a brain performance program. A four-week protocol that addresses all three systems - your Clock, your Battery, and your Off-Switch - so your brain actually recovers at night and you wake up sharp instead of foggy.</p>
    <p class="body-text">The protocol is complete - I've tested it. It's packaged into short daily video lessons, ten to fifteen minutes each, so you can follow it step by step. Every lesson gives you one specific action. All four weeks are recorded and waiting for you. And it's progressive: each week builds on the last. Zero fluff. No "why sleep matters" filler. You already know it matters. This is about getting your hours back.</p>
    <div class="week-card">
      <p class="week-num">Week 1</p>
      <h3>Reset Your Clock</h3>
      <p>You don't pick your mornings - they arrive already decided, and the whole day gets built on whatever they hand you. Some days you'd want an hour to yourself first, some days you'd want to be at the desk in thirty minutes - right now neither one is really a choice.</p>
      <p class="result">The job: show you what's making that decision for you, and give you the two moves that take it back. Neither one is willpower, and you pick the time yourself.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="week-card">
      <p class="week-num">Week 2</p>
      <h3>Charge Your Battery</h3>
      <p>You already run a system for the afternoon. A cutoff you set yourself, a nap you schedule, a lunch built around not crashing, a shelf of things you ordered - all of it hand-built, and you are still paying for the same hours every day.</p>
      <p class="result">The job: show you why the middle of the day goes - and it isn't the lunch. Then what to change, so the afternoon stops being something you buy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="week-card">
      <p class="week-num">Week 3</p>
      <h3>Train Your Off-Switch</h3>
      <p>The laptop closes and the day doesn't. You're at dinner still working through something from the afternoon, and actually sitting down feels like something you have to earn first.</p>
      <p class="result">The job: show you what keeps the day running after you've stopped working, and how to close it. Not discipline - telling yourself to relax is the thing that already didn't work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="week-card">
      <p class="week-num">Week 4</p>
      <h3>Sync All Three</h3>
      <p>Anything you build works right up until the first week that doesn't go to plan - a trip, a deadline, three bad nights in a row. That's usually where people decide the whole thing never worked.</p>
      <p class="result">The job: show you what actually breaks when life interrupts, and what to do to get back - so a bad week costs you a few days, sometimes nothing at all, instead of the whole thing.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="body-text">Remember those eight or ten hours where only three or four are sharp? They start actually counting. Not because you're trying harder, but because your system is working. You get the same hours back - except this time they're real hours. Sharp hours. Think about what those two or three hours are actually worth to you. Not in theory - in real money, in projects you finish, in clients you land.</p>
    <p class="body-text">This isn't biohacking, wellness-influencer advice, or guru wisdom. It's informed by CBT-i, used in clinics worldwide, adapted from the work of a sleep specialist with over thirty years of clinical practice. I'm not selling a personality. I'm sharing research that changed my life. Just the protocol, the research, and what actually works. Four weeks. That's it. Not a six-month commitment - a protocol with an end.</p>
    <p style="text-align:center;margin:8px 0 8px;"><a href="#enroll" class="cta-btn">Enroll Now</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="section proof">
    <p class="label">Before You Decide</p>
    <h2>What I Can Prove, And What I Can't</h2>
    <p class="body-text">I'm Kirill. Everything on this page - the protocol, the lessons, the app - was built in four months, and I built nearly all of it myself. You can open every one of them right now. That is the only thing here you do not have to take my word for.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Four months. Before that, the same kind of work sat at a spoonful a year and never finished. For three years I put in full days and produced almost nothing, and for most of them the word I used for myself was lazy. I had already fixed everything you are supposed to fix - the diet, the exercise, the supplements. I got tested, more than once, and it came back normal every time. Three years of it cost me $70,000 and a career, and it reached the people closest to me long before it reached my work.</p>
    <p class="body-text">By the end I had stopped expecting it to come back. Not "I need a holiday" - I had quietly accepted that whatever it takes to do real work had gone, and that I would sit and watch the last of my savings run out with nothing left in me to stop it. Starting a career again was beyond me. So was building anything of my own.</p>
    <p class="body-text">Then I found what was actually running out. Four months later, you are reading the thing I built.</p>
